Z||000008||maryjane||08-02-2001||09:13 PM||||Richard,the U.S. is dead last in foriegn aid among the top ten industrialized nations. It is the lowest it has been since the start of the Cold War in the late 1940's. We give roughly 1% of our budget as bilateral aid. Of that 1% about half is in the form of direct military aid, either direct weapons or cash to purchase same. Along with the Jewish nation of Israel, we also militarily support Jordan, Egypt, Saudia Arabia,and the Palistinian State. Very few people would immigrate to the U.S. from Israel if such an<BR>ultimatum were issued. Inversely, many American Jews might immigrate to their homeland.They would probably, as a nation, choose to 'go it alone'. They've done it before, and are capable of doing it again. Most of their military hardware nowadays is either 'home grown' or at least foriegn stuff modified and improved to suit their own needs. Cutting off our military aid would have little or no effect on the balance of power in the region. Remember, most countries in the region have vast oil wealth with which to buy weapons. And they buy it from everywhere and anywhere. <BR>I've seen firsthand the civilian loss of life in Viet Nam, I can say it is a terrible thing and I deplore it. Having said that, it is a fact of religion related combat anywhere. It happens in Ireland,the Balkans, India/Pakistan. Our goal from Washington is not to make one side stronger than another, but to try to keep a balance of power. How they use that power is out of our hands. Both the current Bush administration and the Clinton whitehouse have advised Israel to recognize the Palastinian State, and advocated that the PLO not use terrorism in this way.<BR>My .02 cents <p>[This message has been edited by maryjane (edited 08-02-2001).]||||reg||1
